1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,286
Text messages from an Orchard Core site go through an SMS provider.

2
00:00:05,286 --> 00:00:10,101
In this video, you enable the SMS feature, choose the default provider, send

3
00:00:10,101 --> 00:00:15,210
a test message, and set up the Twilio and Azure Communication Services providers.

4
00:00:19,810 --> 00:00:25,005
The SMS feature sends text messages through a provider, the service that delivers them.

5
00:00:25,005 --> 00:00:29,697
Log writes messages to the application log, Twilio sends them through a Twilio

6
00:00:29,697 --> 00:00:33,830
account, and Azure Communication Services through an Azure resource.

7
00:00:34,580 --> 00:00:36,701
One provider is the default.

8
00:00:36,701 --> 00:00:43,000
Workflow tasks, notifications, and two-factor sign-in codes that go by text message all use it.

9
00:00:43,750 --> 00:00:49,336
You choose the default under Settings, Communication, SMS, and check a provider under

10
00:00:49,336 --> 00:00:51,239
Tools, Testing, SMS Test.

11
00:00:51,239 --> 00:00:57,807
Both pages need the Manage SMS Settings permission, and the settings page also needs Manage settings.

12
00:00:57,807 --> 00:00:59,710
Administrators have both.

13
00:01:03,961 --> 00:01:07,223
Let's open Tools, Features, and search for SMS.

14
00:01:07,223 --> 00:01:12,701
The SMS feature is in the Communication category, with an Enable button on its row.

15
00:01:13,172 --> 00:01:14,381
Select Enable.

16
00:01:14,381 --> 00:01:19,155
A message confirms that the feature was enabled, and the admin menu gains

17
00:01:19,155 --> 00:01:21,572
SMS under Settings, Communication.

18
00:01:25,778 --> 00:01:28,399
Open Settings, Communication, SMS.

19
00:01:28,399 --> 00:01:32,462
The Providers tab has one setting, Default SMS provider.

20
00:01:32,462 --> 00:01:36,198
Its list holds only the providers that are enabled.

21
00:01:36,651 --> 00:01:40,522
The list shows only Log, because no other provider is enabled.

22
00:01:40,522 --> 00:01:45,020
The site offers Log only in a development environment, and it writes each

23
00:01:45,020 --> 00:01:48,379
message to the application log instead of sending it.

24
00:01:48,379 --> 00:01:49,631
Let's select it.

25
00:01:50,091 --> 00:01:51,045
Select Save.

26
00:01:51,045 --> 00:01:54,911
The page confirms that the site settings were updated successfully.

27
00:01:59,112 --> 00:02:01,251
Open Tools, Testing, SMS Test.

28
00:02:01,251 --> 00:02:05,589
A test goes through the provider you pick in the Provider list, not

29
00:02:05,589 --> 00:02:09,452
through the default one, and Phone Number is the recipient.

30
00:02:09,914 --> 00:02:13,929
Select Log, then enter a phone number with its country code, as the

31
00:02:13,929 --> 00:02:15,689
hint under the field says.

32
00:02:15,689 --> 00:02:17,284
This number is made up.

33
00:02:17,752 --> 00:02:18,701
Select Send.

34
00:02:18,701 --> 00:02:22,708
The page reports that the test SMS message has been successfully sent.

35
00:02:22,708 --> 00:02:27,031
With the Log provider, that means the message was written to the application

36
00:02:27,031 --> 00:02:28,982
log, and nothing was delivered.

37
00:02:33,185 --> 00:02:38,132
To use Twilio, open the SMS settings again and select the Twilio tab.

38
00:02:38,132 --> 00:02:44,465
Select Enable Twilio Provider, and Twilio Account Info appears with three required fields.

39
00:02:44,929 --> 00:02:49,427
Phone Number is the number that sends the messages, with its country code.

40
00:02:49,427 --> 00:02:53,699
Account SID and Auth Token are the details of your Twilio account, and

41
00:02:53,699 --> 00:02:55,836
the token is hidden as you type.

42
00:02:55,836 --> 00:02:57,579
These values are made up.

43
00:02:58,033 --> 00:03:01,113
Select Save, and the page confirms the update.

44
00:03:01,113 --> 00:03:05,260
The Auth Token field is empty again, but the text inside it says

45
00:03:05,260 --> 00:03:07,334
the value is securely stored:

46
00:03:07,334 --> 00:03:11,303
enter a new value to update it, or leave it blank to keep it.

47
00:03:11,760 --> 00:03:14,197
Let's send a test message with Twilio.

48
00:03:14,197 --> 00:03:17,909
The values are made up, so the page reports that the test SMS

49
00:03:17,909 --> 00:03:19,516
message failed to send.

50
00:03:19,516 --> 00:03:23,450
With real values, success means that Twilio accepted the message.

51
00:03:27,684 --> 00:03:31,527
Azure Communication Services has a feature of its own.

52
00:03:31,527 --> 00:03:37,228
In Tools, Features, let's enable Azure Communication Services SMS, which adds a tab

53
00:03:37,228 --> 00:03:38,894
to the SMS settings.

54
00:03:39,379 --> 00:03:44,179
On the Azure Communication Services tab, select Enable, enter the Phone Number that

55
00:03:44,179 --> 00:03:49,087
sends the messages and the Connection String of your Azure resource, and select Save.

56
00:03:49,087 --> 00:03:50,759
These values are made up.

57
00:03:53,553 --> 00:03:58,794
Azure joins Twilio and Log in the Default SMS provider list on the Providers tab.

58
00:03:58,794 --> 00:04:03,613
To send the site's messages through one of them, select it there and save.

59
00:04:05,613 --> 00:04:09,980
Once a default provider is chosen, the features that send text messages use

60
00:04:09,980 --> 00:04:13,862
it, and the SMS Test page checks a provider before you rely on it.

61
00:04:13,862 --> 00:04:15,263
Thanks for watching.

